# G065 — Reservation System

*v2. See `reservation-system.md` for prose: resource conflict check is the
hot path, least-loaded selection strategy, booking as transactional claim.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Conflict-Free Calendar Assignment

[assign-agent-window @t0 @t1 @capability
    IN ORDER TO
        pick the least-loaded qualified agent for a time window
        and book them conflict-free or resist with the reason

    @candidates <- [rs[available {start: @t0, end: @t1, kind: @capability}]]

    @candidates
        where count at least 1
        || -> #no-agents-available

    @selected <- @candidates chosen-by "least-loaded"

    @reservation <- [rs[book {resource-id: @selected,
                                customer-id: @choreography-id,
                                start: @t0, end: @t1}]]
                    <- [rs[conflicts {resource-id: @selected,
                                        start: @t0, end: @t1}]]
                        where result is empty
                    || -> #booking-conflict @resistance:reason

    -> @reservation
]

---

## Verb Surface

[rs
    new
    add-resource       {resource}
    book               {resource-id, customer-id, start, end}    ;; || conflict
    cancel             {reservation-id}
    conflicts          {resource-id, start, end, except?}
    available          {start, end, kind?}
    occupancy          {resource-id}
    customer-bookings  {customer-id}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: conflict verification as `<-` gate

[rs[book {resource-id: @r, ...}]
    <- [rs[conflicts {resource-id: @r, start: @s, end: @e}]]
        where result is empty
]

The `<-` verification gate runs the conflict check between the book
commission and the commit. Failure surfaces as resistance with the
conflicting reservation in the reason.

---

## Selection strategy as bare-word string

[@candidates chosen-by "least-loaded"
    where
        "least-loaded" picks the resource with the fewest bookings in the window
        and "earliest" picks by ascending start time
        and "round-robin" cycles by a counter on the resolver
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @rs <- [rs[new]]
    [rs[add-resource {resource: {id: "A1", kind: "forex"}}]]
    [rs[add-resource {resource: {id: "A2", kind: "forex"}}]]

    @r <- [rs[book {resource-id: "A1", customer-id: "C1",
                     start: @tomorrow-9am, end: @tomorrow-10am}]]
    -> @r:status        ;; -> "booked"

    ;; Overlapping booking on A1 rejects
    [rs[book {resource-id: "A1", customer-id: "C2",
               start: @tomorrow-9_30am, end: @tomorrow-11am}]]
        || -> "conflict"

    ;; Overlapping on A2 succeeds
    @r2 <- [rs[book {resource-id: "A2", customer-id: "C2",
                      start: @tomorrow-9_30am, end: @tomorrow-11am}]]
    -> @r2:status       ;; -> "booked"
]
